[Music]  [Music]  well i think the pandemic ranks  obviously  hugely on everybody's minds  and i think what's unusual about the  pandemic is that it has been obviously  first and foremost um it's a you know  it's a medical  issue in economic terms it's both a  demand and a supply shock as in it's  affected um obviously all of our lives  and that's the demand side of the  economy it's also been hugely disruptive  to the supply side if we think about  borders having closed so in my book the  great economist how their ideas can help  us today  i look at the past 250 years really  since the industrial revolution and  there have been a number of crises  obviously financial crises of 10 years  ago comes to mind um but having a  pandemic  generate a crisis recession  that's actually fairly unusual on a  global basis in fact the spanish flu of  1918 is really the only comparable one  and i think that probably um you know  just gives a sense of how unusual um  this time is  [Music]  well there's a lot of i think different  ideas we can glean from a great  economist um you know over uh the past  couple of centuries or so so just help  us think through the kinds of policies  that are needed uh to cushion um you  know the economic impact um of this  pandemic so for instance  if we look at  how  just how devastating it's been for a lot  of people's livelihoods this idea of  using furlough schemes  income support to support viable  businesses keeping people attached uh to  the labor force helping them um you know  just maintain employment even though  there's been this massive demand shock a  lot of those ideas can be traced to the  1930s so this is the era of the  keynesian revolution john maynard keynes  and  some of his disciples like john john  robinson they really focused on  how  the classical economist so this is  people who came before them people like  david ricardo  didn't think  i think enough about how you get the  economy back to full employment to get  to people back on their feet so the  keynesian revolution the 1930s was  predominantly aimed at trying to cushion  um and get  the uk economy back after the the great  depression by focusing on employment by  focusing on how government can invest  in order uh to try and just get the  economy back to where it was  more quickly and that's where also where  you get that very well known saying by  keynes  you know in the long run we're all dead  and so waiting for the economy to write  itself which was the classical economic  approach um he thought  probably wasn't enough  [Music]  so a lot of the criticism  of the keynesian approach in the 1930s  was the amount of debt that governments  ended up  carrying as a result of massive  investment charges in the uk but also in  the united states for instance the new  deal so even though there was investment  in infrastructure  it was still the case that you would get  returns later on  but you would end up with higher debt  levels  now so if you fast forward to today  there is a similar concern that  debt levels were already high because  that  from the financial crisis a decade ago  had left us with a fairly highly um a  higher level um than obviously we had  seen before so you're starting from a  high level and then you're adding more  government borrowing on top of that but  where i would see perhaps um a  difference in stress or nuance is that  you know the 1930s and in fact the 1920s  which was building back from the first  world war as well as the spanish flu of  1918  uh meant that  you had this sense of you hear the term  roaring twenties that wasn't quite what  it was really um in britain but you got  the sense that there was plenty of  demand and there was this feeling that  as economies rebuild you would get a  sense  that could that there's economic growth  we're actually in a period where yes you  can also have pent-up demand and you do  see that with lockdown easing you see  the boost in economic activity but our  past decade or so in fact even further  um before that has been characterized by  a concern over slow growth  and slow  wage growth so for instance in the uk  median wages and once you adjust for  inflation hasn't increased really  for about a decade in the united states  it's hasn't increased since the 1970s  so we're actually in a period where the  expectation is growth is too slow so  this context this difference makes a  massive i think difference in thinking  about what it means to invest well also  and lots of different people have  described it in different ways on the  cusp of perhaps another digital  industrial revolution so the 21st  century world requires different  infrastructure than what the 20th  century world  had given us so all of those things  point to if government were to borrow to  invest  in say digital infrastructure in human  capital which is one of the key things  to make any digital economy work and  give us that ability to adapt then you  can generate  growth in areas that's unlikely to  address both the slow growth context  we've come out from but also position  ourselves for what the 21st century  world you know uh could mean and it's  not just services or e-commerce it also  has effects on um you know what we  produce a lot of manufactured goods you  know it's hardware as well as software  it's also affected by  the changes in technology  as well as agriculture so what that  suggests is if government can invest  in these areas and in people  and then yes debt levels will  be either already high but it could  generate growth in the coming years  that  help  help i think us recover from the  pandemic um better  so as with like mark twain said history  doesn't repeat itself but it often  rhymes so i think we can learn lessons  from what didn't work so well um back in  the interwar years and think about the  ways to um  you know to  to do it better this time  [Music]  so my book the great economist um  focused on an earlier vintage of great  economists and the only great economist  um in the book who's still alive today  is robert solo and he's working in his  90s so i start with adam smith i go  through dave ricardo germain arcanes  we've discussed and also a number of  great economists from the 20th century  they were selected on the basis that  they may not have been the first but  they were certainly the ones that came  up with the seminal model so for  instance david ricardo  came up with the theory of international  trade that still governs the way that we  think about trade even as that theory  has been developed and evolved and  changed with circumstances and you know  since he wrote it in the 19th century  so if i were to look ahead 100 years and  apply that same criteria  and again my book is focused on you know  the big picture growth uh economic  systems and development  uh the nobel prize the house prize in  economics was recently awarded to  william nordhaus for his work on um  incorporating the environment into  economic models so i would see that as  you know a b  uh issue of course for the next century  and the great economists  who  develop  ways in which we can grow in a greener  way in a way that  protects the planet  and fashions policies so that you know  we can have a great quality of life and  not just the quantity of growth i see  that as one of the major  you know  determinants of who will be great  in the next hundred years  [Music]  yeah that was actually one of the most  fun parts of writing the book um every  book um is about uh not just the ideas  but about the great economists  themselves  and you know every chapter  focuses on the ideas of a great  economist where those ideas came from  how it's rooted  in their times and then  how that applies to the way that we are  thinking about our big economic  challenges um today so i would say the  similarity that um i drew across these  great economists  was they were all very  passionate about contributing to the  debates of the day  there are um you know  economists who work and are very  focused on on their research but these  great economists i think really  contributed to policy debates so um you  know adam smith he wrote the wealth of  nations in 1776 he timed it to influence  the english position on the american  independence the war of independence in  1776  um david ricardo he lit he became an  mp and contributed to the repeal of the  coin laws which finally happened after  his death in 1846 which saw his theory  of comparative advantage  went out over the existing theories  which were around really protectionism  mercantilism that corn laws were this  protectionist piece of agricultural  legislation  and you know this continues um into the  20th century we talked about kings and  uh the work that he did i'm not just in  terms of  rethinking the role of government  in writing the economy in the short term  he was also influential in developing  the bretton woods institutions and this  is named after  the meeting in bretton woods new  hampshire which  formed the international monetary fund  the world bank so essentially the  international organizations that are  still with us um today  and then in the postwar period uh the  great economists  i think were really engaged in what i  described as a battle of ideas  so  in the postwar period there was um you  know so i'll use um joseph schumpeter as  the example he's best known for his  theory of creative destruction which  helps us understand and he studied  american massive companies at the turn  of the 20th century as well as the  mitchell stand in germany  so his theories around how firms  innovate and then this darwinian process  of creative destruction that's what he's  best known for in terms of his research  his technical work but his best known  book was probably his 1942 book and that  was called  capitalism socialism and democracy and  what he did was he contributed to that  battle of ideas of that period where you  saw this rise of marxism you saw this  rise of communism and socialist systems  and  those who believed in a capitalist or  market-based economy  like him made the case for how the  capitalist system needed reform he  described it like an engine if you don't  maintain the capitalist engine it could  stall  or certainly run badly  and that contribution to this to this  debate because remember the early 20th  century um was the rise of the soviet  union and that led to the cold war and  the postwar period china became  communists in 1949 there was a real  difference in terms of the economic  system  and he and other great economists  contributed to the um to the debates and  that eventually led to  the welfare state which we saw emerge in  the early 20th century and i think  really took root in the post-war period  which of course in this country gave us  the national health system and elsewhere  created a welfare system  and  you know that took quite a long time in  terms of changing  the capitalist system say from the days  of adam smith around factories and no  real social safety net so the changes in  ideas in the late victorian period when  there was a focus on the deserving poor  on greater philanthropy charitable  causes  all that eventually fed into welfare  state capitalism which is the system  that we have today  um  so the great economist which um you know  engaged in those issues even though i'm  taking joseph schampeter as an example  you know the um i'm pretty sure the  model in  you would consider the model that he  used in that book is probably not as  technical as the model for his creative  destruction work um however um you know  that that's not a deterrent to engaging  with the big issues and i think that's  what characterized and the great  economist and throughout history and of  course the other thing i always found  and you know hugely interesting when i  was writing the book is that they  disagreed massively so you have on the  one hand you already mentioned that  marxism but just even keynesianism  versus more free market economists like  friedrich kayak who was writing around  the same time as joseph schumpeter and  his ideas around the free market not  only influenced those behind the iron  curtain that was also linked to  thatcherism and the reagan revolution of  the 1980s so you have this battle of  ideas um  you know even between economists who are  all working generally towards reforming  the market-based economy the capitalist  system but from very different  approaches  and you know history proves them right  for a period wrong for a period and  we're still looking at um you know what  has uh what what has worked and what  needs uh adaptation and that's where you  know i sort of conclude the book which  is looking you know ahead at some of  these issues some of which we've already  touched upon and um  and thinking about how we can learn  lessons from what these great economists  and you know went through and apply it  to today but you know we're able to to  pick and choose because they've left  such a rich  legacy the other thing i would say is  you know um the biographies are probably  some of my favorite parts of writing the  book because they just led such hugely  interesting lives you know divorces  affairs um  ambition uh you know  it just it you know had it all  you know um so the great accounts are  hugely interesting people um as well as  having made a massive impact on you know  on the world  for more debates talks and interviews  subscribe today to the institute of arts  and ideas  at iai tv  you

